course: dictionary
lesson: 8647
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Seam is one of them. The words around it are its context, and they tell you which meaning the writer means. Read the whole sentence, then ask: which meaning fits here?
explain: «Do not eat from a can that is swollen or badly dented along its seams.» Cans have joins along their edges, so seams here are the joined lines on the metal. The clues swollen and dented tell you the seams are on the can itself.
explain: «The skull has 22 bones: 8 around the brain and 14 in the face, joined by zigzag seams called sutures.» Here seams are the joins where two bones meet. A seam is always the place where two things are joined together.
explain: Seam can also be a verb. To seam something is to join it with a seam. Phrases like «bursting at the seams» are different again: they mean very full. When a word feels strange, check the clues first.
fact: A seam is the line where two pieces of cloth, leather or other material are sewn or joined together.
fact: A seam is also a thin layer of coal, gold or another mineral in rock underground.
fact: In cricket, the seam is the raised stitching round the ball.
fact: «Bursting at the seams» means very full. «Come apart at the seams» means break or fall apart. «Seamless» means smooth, with no joins.
fact: To seam something means to join it with a seam, so the word can be a verb as well as a noun.
exercise: «Do not eat from a can that is swollen or badly dented along its seams.» In this sentence, what does seam mean? | *The joined lines along the edges of the can | A thin layer of coal in rock | A bag full of books | The stitching round a cricket ball
exercise: In «The skull has 22 bones: 8 around the brain and 14 in the face, joined by zigzag seams called sutures», what do the seams join? | *Bones | Layers of coal in rock | Pieces of leather | Thin sheets of gold
exercise: Hannah says her old bag is about to come apart at the seams. What does she mean? | It is made of shiny leather | It is bursting with books and full to the top | *It is about to break or fall apart | It needs a new zip
test: «The bag was bursting at the seams with books.» What does bursting at the seams mean? | *Very full | Painted bright red | Very empty | Torn in two
test: Tom needs a needle to seam the torn trousers. What does seam mean in this sentence? | To dig for coal | To fill up with books | *To join with stitches | To throw a fast ball
exam: Which phrase in these words means very full? | Come apart at the seams | Seamless | Seam bowler | *Bursting at the seams
